{"t":"2026-10-22T12:02:41.438Z","event":"finish","launch":"0477-fact-checker","n":11,"role":"fact-checker","ok":true,"reason":null,"tokens":111998,"cumulative":2537263,"cost":1.8388,"turns":44,"minutes":4.85,"sessions":["S-10"],"abandoned":[],"denials":0,"said":"I checked four lessons this session. Three went back to the builder (revise) and one passed. The session closed when it reached its cap of 4.\n\n| # | Lesson | Outcome | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | ant-on-a-cube | **revise** | c1, c3 and c5 verified. My own shortest-path search over the cube surface gives √5 ≈ 2.236, and the drawn routes and unfolded faces match the claims. **c2 disputed:** it says any two faces that share an edge unfold with the ant's corners at opposite ends, which holds for only 6 of the 12 pairs. **c4 disputed:** \"the shortest walk is a straight line on some unfolding\" holds for boxes but not for every surface made of flat faces. The reveal's \"the best walk over a surface is found by unfolding it\" is wider still, since a sphere can't be unfolded. |\n| 2 | slice-a-corner-off | **revise** | c1, c3 and c4 verified. **c2 disputed:** the reveal's \"always adds k − 1 corners, k edges, 1 face\" fails when the cut passes through other corners. Cutting a cube corner through its three neighbouring corners gives 7 corners, 12 edges and 7 faces. The fix is wording only. |\n| 3 | friends-of-friends-overlap | **revise** | c1, c2 and c3 verified; my own count around the ring gives 10, 10, 10, then 31 within 3 steps, and 100 for the farthest person. **c4 left unverified:** the network blocks both online copies of the Easley & Kleinberg textbook. Search results suggest the caution it cites is in chapter 20, not chapter 2. \"Much smaller\" also overstates it. Someone with the book needs to confirm the source. |\n| 4 | new-friend-loosens-the-circle | **pass** | All 3 claims verified, and the drawn links match each readout. I confirmed the chapter 3 definition through a course copy of the chapter that showed up in search results. The section number (3.1) is still unconfirmed, but it isn't shown to learners. |\n\nEach record is at `company/reviews/<slug>/2026-10-22-fact-check.md` in that lesson's worktree.\n\nWhat confused me:\n- The company's CLAUDE.md asks every session to update the board and the progress log. Your instructions override that, so I left both alone.\n- For a source I couldn't open, the rules say the claim stays unverified, which forces a revise. The fix then needs a person with the book, not the builder. I flagged this in the findings.\n- Two of my three revises were about wording that states a general rule, not wrong numbers."}
