{"t":"2026-10-22T13:05:10.444Z","event":"finish","launch":"1256-demo-builder","n":23,"role":"demo-builder","ok":true,"reason":null,"tokens":77299,"cumulative":1077271,"cost":0.7228,"turns":17,"minutes":2.13,"sessions":["S-27"],"abandoned":[],"denials":0,"said":"I handed in two items, both fact-check revises. The session then closed at its cap of 2.\n\n1. **O-7, regions-from-circle-points (now v2).** The fact-checker flagged \"so the count was never doubling\" as false, because the counts 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 do double. I changed it in both the reveal in `Demo.tsx` and claim c2 to \"so the rule was never doubling: it only matched doubling up to 5 points.\" All four claims are back to `unverified`, with checkedBy, checkedOn and the fact-checker's notes removed. I added `## Pre-flight v2` to `build.md`. `meta check --order=O-7` passed, and the order went on to test1. The reveal grows from 69 to about 75 words, under the 90 cap. I did not re-run reveal-fit, so the qa stage will be the first to measure it.\n\n2. **O-1, push-the-frame-over (now v3).** Claim c3 said one cut-and-slide turns any parallelogram into a rectangle, which is false when the top overhangs by more than the base. I limited c3 to this frame: its overhang is 3 cos θ, at most 3, which is less than the base of 4. The learner-facing text already talks about this frame only, so `Demo.tsx` is unchanged. All claims are back to `unverified`. I added a Revise v3 section and `## Pre-flight v3` to `build.md`. `meta check --order=O-1` passed, and the order went on to test1.\n\n**What confused me:** I cut the `room next` output to its last 30 lines, so I lost the second item's header (order id and workdir). I found it in `state.json` (R-52 → O-1) and took the workdir from the memory entries naming push-the-frame-over. Next time I'll keep the start of that output."}
{"t":"2026-10-22T13:06:22.839Z","event":"finish","launch":"1204-fact-checker","n":20,"role":"fact-checker","ok":true,"reason":null,"tokens":97223,"cumulative":1646910,"cost":1.4234,"turns":35,"minutes":5.55,"sessions":["S-24"],"abandoned":[],"denials":0,"said":"Session S-24 is over: I checked 4 items, which hit the cap. Two passed and two went back to build.\n\n1. **pick-two-or-pick-four (O-9): pass.** Both claims are verified. I recomputed C(6,k) = 1, 6, 15, 20, 15, 6, 1 and C(10,2) = C(10,8) = 45. The going/staying matching is correct, and the pair chips on the stage are drawn in the order the code lists them. It moves on to the learner stage.\n2. **regions-from-circle-points (O-7), first check: revise.** The numbers are all right: 31 pieces with 15 separate crossings at 6 points, and 30 pieces for evenly spaced points. But claim c2 and the reveal said \"the count was never doubling\" right after \"Doubling held up to 5 points\", and as written that is false. I disputed c2 and asked for the sentence to talk about the rule, not the count.\n3. **push-the-frame-over (O-1), its third check: revise.** Claims c1, c2 and c4 are right (heights and areas at 90°, 60° and 30°, perimeter 14, and the recall answer 5). Claim c3 says cutting the triangle off \"a parallelogram\" and sliding it across always makes a rectangle. That fails when the top overhangs the base by more than the base's length: for example base 1 with sides 3 at 30°. It does work for this frame, so I asked for c3 to be limited to it.\n4. **regions-from-circle-points (O-7), recheck: pass.** The builder changed only that sentence, now \"the rule was never doubling: it only matched doubling up to 5 points\". I compared against my first check and re-ran my script, and all 4 claims are verified. It moves on to the learner stage.\n\nThings that confused me:\n- **The first leave was refused.** My record table had an \"Old status\" column, and the claims check read the first status word in each row, so it saw `unverified` against `verified` in meta.json. Dropping that column fixed it, and I saved this as a learned line.\n- **Order O-1 was on its third check,** so a fact-check record already existed. Following an earlier learned note, I wrote the new record as `…-fact-check-v2.md` and did not open the old one before writing my findings.\n- **The two revises are judgement calls about wording,** not wrong numbers: the \"never doubling\" sentence and c3 stated more generally than it holds. I chose revise because my notes from earlier sessions say to check the claim exactly as written. Whoever decides on these orders may think it was strict."}
{"t":"2026-10-22T13:11:55.679Z","event":"finish","launch":"1222-learner-evaluator","n":21,"role":"learner-evaluator","ok":true,"reason":null,"tokens":95831,"cumulative":910965,"cost":0.8739,"turns":24,"minutes":10.09,"sessions":["S-25"],"abandoned":[],"denials":0,"said":"I reviewed two lessons and both got a launch verdict. Both moved on to the launch review, and the session closed because nothing else was queued.\n\n1. **pick-two-or-pick-four (O-9, v1): launch.** The first tap of \"Next pair\" shows a pair going and the four friends left behind, and the ending explanation says each pair matches exactly one four. I checked all 15 small chips in the pair grid against the go/stay colours: every pair appears once. Two notes, neither blocking:\n   - The chips are rows of unlabeled dots, so the learner has to work out that each dot position stands for one of the friends A to F.\n   - The ending says groups of 3 are \"the most\" (20 ways), but the lesson never shows the other group sizes for comparison.\n\n   Review: `company/reviews/pick-two-or-pick-four/2026-10-22-learner-counted-pairs-no-complement.md`\n\n2. **regions-from-circle-points (O-7, v2): launch.** The doubling pattern breaks at 6 points (31 pieces, not 32) in the learner's own table. The rule in the ending, pieces = 1 + lines + crossings, can be checked against every column. I counted all 15 crossing dots in the 6-point drawing and none has three lines meeting. Three notes, neither blocking:\n   - 31 pieces are too many to count by hand on a phone, so the learner has to trust the readout.\n   - \"Crossings come from groups of four\" is stated rather than shown.\n   - A learner who picks 30 is never told why 30 is wrong.\n\n   Review: `company/reviews/regions-from-circle-points/2026-10-22-learner-pattern-trusting-counter.md`\n\n**What confused me:** each review has to name the lesson's version, but the version appears only in `meta.json`, which this stage may not read, and not in the walkthrough. I guessed v1 both times. That was right for the first lesson. The leave check refused the second, which is v2, and I corrected the line once the refusal named the version. That lesson's first walkthrough has no earlier version on record, so v1 was a reasonable guess that turned out wrong."}
{"t":"2026-10-22T13:12:33.175Z","event":"finish","launch":"1236-head-of-product","n":22,"role":"head-of-product","ok":true,"reason":null,"tokens":92839,"cumulative":831590,"cost":0.8415,"turns":15,"minutes":9.74,"sessions":["S-26"],"abandoned":[],"denials":0,"said":"I gave both lessons a **go**, and both have moved on to the ship gates. After the second item the queue was empty, so the session closed (2 of the 4 allowed).\n\n1. **pick-two-or-pick-four (v1, order O-9).** Both claims are verified, QA passed on phone and desktop, and the learner reviewer said launch. The record is `company/rituals/records/2026-10-22-launch-review-pick-two-or-pick-four.md`. Objections carried forward for a v2:\n   - The pair chips (rows of dots, no letters) have no key explaining which dot is which friend.\n   - The reveal says \"Groups of 3 are the most\", but the lesson never shows the counts for the other group sizes.\n   - The \"Why trust this?\" sheet opens a sentence with \"Recall:\", which makes no sense to someone who hasn't reached the recall question yet.\n   - The picture switches from E,F to A,B between the last explore step and the reveal.\n   - The empty learner-review link in meta.json, plus the three standing ones: no analytics predictions, accessibility review advisory only, and the learning-designer merged into the builder.\n\n2. **regions-from-circle-points (v2, order O-7).** The fact-checker's v1 finding was a false sentence, \"the count was never doubling\". v2 fixed it in both the reveal and the claim text. All four claims are verified on v2, and QA and the learner reviewer passed v2. The record is `company/rituals/records/2026-10-22-launch-review-regions-from-circle-points.md`. The lesson differs from the brief in two places, and I judged both improvements: it uses a table instead of a one-line readout, and the question adds \"unevenly so no three lines meet\". Objections carried forward:\n   - A learner who picks 30 is never shown why 30 is wrong; 30 is what evenly spaced points give.\n   - \"Crossings come from groups of four\" is stated, not shown.\n   - \"Count the pieces\" is impractical with 31 pieces on a phone.\n   - The question's number list is hard to read.\n   - The image description says \"1 points\".\n   - The same \"Recall:\" wording and empty learner-review link as above, plus the three standing objections.\n\n**Confusing:**\n- The second item came from a different order with its own worktree. I wrote its record in that order's worktree, even though this session's stated workdir was the pick-two one.\n- Both walkthroughs' \"Why trust this?\" sheets were recorded before the fact-check, so they still say NOT YET CHECKED. I judged claim status from meta.json instead."}
